Portable libumem.
================

This is a port of Solaris libumem to non-Solaris systems.

The port was made while integrating libumem with our Ecelerity MTA product, so
your initial experience will not be 100% out-of-the-box, because there is no
standalone configure script for the library at this time. (patches welcome!)

In addition, since our deployment is threaded, we force the library into
threaded mode.

While the library is itself stable (it's the memory allocator used by the
Solaris OS), the port may have a few rough edges.  We're shipping umem with
Linux and Windows versions of our product as we have found it to be stable.

We will continue to update this project as and when we make improvements, and
welcome third-party patches that improve the usability for everyone.


Wez Furlong,
Message Systems, Inc.
wez (at) messagesystems (dot) com


Per-Thread Caching (PTC)
========================

Overview
--------

Per-thread caching (PTC) is a lock-free optimization for malloc() and free()
that eliminates the per-CPU lock acquisition normally required by libumem for
each allocation. When contention is low and malloc/free frequency is high,
the lock overhead can dominate execution time. PTC alleviates this by caching
recently freed buffers in thread-local storage for reuse by subsequent
allocations within the same thread.

### How it works

Each thread maintains a private `tmem_t` structure containing:

- `tm_size`: the total number of bytes currently cached by this thread
- `tm_roots[16]`: an array of 16 linked-list heads, one per cache size

Each root corresponds to a umem cache. When free() is called, instead of
returning the buffer to the umem cache (which requires a lock), the buffer
is placed on the appropriate per-thread linked list. When malloc() is called,
the per-thread list is checked first; if a buffer is available, it is returned
directly without any locking.

When a thread exits, all buffers in its per-thread cache are returned to the
appropriate umem caches.

### Allocation size coverage

PTC covers the first 16 umem cache sizes. The exact sizes depend on whether
the system is 32-bit or 64-bit:

**64-bit (LP64):** allocations up to 448 bytes
  - Cache sizes: 8, 16, 32, 48, 64, 80, 96, 112, 128, 160, 192, 224, 256,
    320, 384, 448

**32-bit (ILP32):** allocations up to 256 bytes
  - Cache sizes: 8, 16, 24, 32, 40, 48, 56, 64, 80, 96, 112, 128, 160,
    192, 224, 256

Allocations larger than these thresholds fall through to the standard
libumem allocation path.

### How PTC is implemented

At initialization time, libumem dynamically generates optimized assembly
implementations of malloc() and free() (called `ptcmalloc` and `ptcfree`)
that are patched into the PLT (Procedure Linkage Table). These generated
functions handle the per-thread cache operations directly in a small number
of instructions, falling back to the standard libumem malloc/free when
the allocation is not serviceable by the per-thread cache.


Platform Support
----------------

PTC requires architecture-specific assembly code generation. The following
platforms are supported:

| Architecture | PTC Support | Notes                            |
|--------------|-------------|----------------------------------|
| x86_64       | Full        | amd64 genasm implementation      |
| i386         | Full        | i386 genasm implementation       |
| SPARC        | None        | No genasm implementation         |
| ARM/AArch64  | None        | No genasm implementation         |
| Other        | None        | Not implemented                  |

On unsupported architectures, libumem falls back to the standard malloc/free
path with no per-thread caching. The library remains fully functional; only
the PTC optimization is unavailable.


Configuration
-------------

PTC is configured through the `UMEM_OPTIONS` environment variable using
the `perthread_cache` option, which sets the maximum number of bytes each
thread is allowed to cache.

### Enable with default cache size (1 MB per thread)

PTC is enabled by default on supported architectures. No configuration
is needed:

    # PTC is active automatically on x86/x86_64
    ./my_application

### Set a custom per-thread cache size

The value accepts optional size suffixes (k, m, g):

    # 512 KB per-thread cache
    UMEM_OPTIONS=perthread_cache=512k ./my_application

    # 2 MB per-thread cache
    UMEM_OPTIONS=perthread_cache=2m ./my_application

    # Exact byte value (262144 bytes = 256 KB)
    UMEM_OPTIONS=perthread_cache=262144 ./my_application

### Disable per-thread caching

    # Disable PTC by setting cache size to zero
    UMEM_OPTIONS=perthread_cache=0 ./my_application

### Combine with other UMEM_OPTIONS

Options are separated by commas:

    # Custom cache size with mmap backend
    UMEM_OPTIONS=perthread_cache=512k,backend=mmap ./my_application


When to Use PTC
---------------

PTC provides the greatest benefit when:

- **High-frequency small allocations:** The application performs many
  malloc/free calls per second for objects <= 448 bytes (64-bit) or
  <= 256 bytes (32-bit).

- **Multi-threaded workloads:** Multiple threads are allocating and freeing
  memory concurrently. PTC eliminates lock contention on the per-CPU
  magazine locks for small allocations.

- **Allocation-free hot paths are impractical:** When object pooling or
  arena allocation are not options and the application must rely on
  malloc/free in performance-critical sections.

PTC is less beneficial or unnecessary when:

- The application primarily allocates large objects (above the PTC size
  thresholds).

- The application is single-threaded (lock contention is not an issue).

- Memory usage must be kept to an absolute minimum (PTC adds per-thread
  overhead).


Limitations
-----------

- **Debug mode disables PTC.** When `UMEM_DEBUG` is set, PTC is
  automatically disabled to allow the full debugging infrastructure
  (auditing, guards, redzone checking) to function correctly.

- **nomagazines disables PTC.** Setting `nomagazines` in `UMEM_OPTIONS`
  disables all caching including PTC.

- **Architecture restrictions.** PTC is only available on x86 and x86_64.
  On other architectures, libumem operates normally but without the
  per-thread caching optimization.

- **Per-thread memory overhead.** Each thread can cache up to
  `perthread_cache` bytes (default 1 MB). In applications with many threads,
  this can result in significant memory being held in per-thread caches
  rather than being returned to the system. Tune `perthread_cache` downward
  if this is a concern.

- **No runtime reconfiguration.** Once libumem is initialized, the PTC
  cache size cannot be changed without restarting the process.

- **Thread exit releases cached memory.** Buffers cached by a thread are
  only returned to the umem caches when that thread exits. Long-lived
  threads will hold onto their cached memory for the duration of their
  lifetime.

